+\paragraph{A nine-player example.}
+For $\ell=1$, the three sectors each have labels $0,1,2$. Start with
+$A=B=C=I_3$ and set $A_{01}=2$, $B_{02}=1$, and $C_{12}=2$.
+The proposition gives $T=9$ and $H_i=3$ for all nine players, so every
+protected floor is one. Paying all nine floors costs nine but gives only
+three to the special team $(0,1,2)$, whose value is five. Covering this
+team and paying the six unmatched players their floors therefore requires
+at least $5+6=11$ in total.
+
+\begin{table}[htbp]
+\centering
+\caption{A minimum-cost protected allocation for $\ell=1$;
+columns identify sectors and rows identify player labels.}
+\label{tab:small-subsidy}
+\begin{tabular}{lccc}\toprule
+Label & Sector 1 & Sector 2 & Sector 3\\\midrule
+$0$ & $3$ & $1$ & $1$\\
+$1$ & $1$ & $1$ & $1$\\
+$2$ & $1$ & $1$ & $1$\\\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+Table~\ref{tab:small-subsidy} attains this lower bound: the special team
+receives five, and every other team has value at most three and receives
+at least three. Thus the subsidy is exactly two, or $2/9$ of original
+worth. The extra budget covers the conflict between the fixed individual
+floors and team stability; the allocation totals eleven and is not a point
+of the original total-nine core. The benchmarks remain those of that
+original core.
+
